// =====================================================================================
// ATH-009 : Macro-enabled document opened via email client
// Severity: MEDIUM
// Pandas  : src/ath/hunting/rules/initial_access_rules.py :: OfficeMacroAttachmentOpened
//
// PROVENANCE: this rule was proposed as a v1/v2 candidate pair by the
// detection-engineering harness (src/ath/engineering/candidates.py), measured on this
// project's own telemetry, and promoted after tightening removed a measured false
// positive. See docs/detection-engineering.md for the full numbers:
//   v1 (any Office app opened by Outlook, no extension check): 1 TP, 29 FP, precision 0.03
//   v2 (macro-capable extension required):                     1 TP,  0 FP, precision 1.00
//
// Fires ONE STEP EARLIER in the kill chain than ATH-001: it catches the attachment
// being opened at all, independent of whether the embedded macro subsequently
// executes -- giving visibility even when a macro is blocked by policy or declined.
//
// KQL notes:
//   has_any            fast term-indexed multi-extension check
//   endswith           not used here on purpose: the extension can appear mid-path in
//                      quoted command lines, so a substring/regex check is safer than
//                      a strict suffix match against ProcessCommandLine
// =====================================================================================

let LookBack = 7d;
let OfficeApps = dynamic(["winword.exe", "excel.exe", "powerpnt.exe", "outlook.exe"]);
// A plain .docx cannot contain a macro at all -- this is a fact about the Office Open
// XML container format, not a threshold tuned to any particular dataset.
let MacroExtensions = dynamic([".docm", ".dotm", ".xlsm", ".xlsb", ".pptm", ".ppsm"]);
DeviceProcessEvents
| where Timestamp > ago(LookBack)
| where InitiatingProcessFileName =~ "outlook.exe"
| where FileName in~ (OfficeApps)
| where ProcessCommandLine has_any (MacroExtensions)
| project
    Timestamp, DeviceName, AccountName, FileName,
    ProcessCommandLine, InitiatingProcessFileName, ReportId
| sort by Timestamp asc

// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// REQUIRED TELEMETRY FIELDS (our synthetic schema)
//   process_events: timestamp, device, user, process_name, parent_process_name,
//            command_line, event_id
//
// SYNTHETIC SCHEMA vs MICROSOFT DEFENDER / SENTINEL
// Direct DeviceProcessEvents mapping, same as ATH-001. Real deployments would ideally
// join this against email-gateway logs (e.g. Microsoft 365 Defender's EmailAttachmentInfo)
// to confirm the file actually arrived by email rather than, say, a USB drive or a
// download -- our synthetic schema has no email telemetry, so this rule infers "email
// client" purely from Outlook being the parent process, which is a reasonable proxy but
// not direct proof of the delivery mechanism.
//
// LIKELY FALSE POSITIVES
//   - Legitimate business documents distributed in a macro-capable format (e.g. .xlsm
//     calculation templates) without containing an actively malicious macro.
//   - Internal mail-merge or reporting workflows that routinely email .xlsm/.docm files.
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// pandas equivalent:
//   mask = (parent == "outlook.exe") & child.isin(OFFICE_APPS)
//   is_macro = candidates["command_line"].str.contains(MACRO_EXTENSIONS_PATTERN)
